# BIAL runtime env manifest. The platform injects these — and ONLY these — into the sandbox at
# provision, and re-injects them on snapshot restore. These are NAMES only: real values live in
# process env, never in a committed file.
#
# How the names reach `next dev`: the supervisor's child-env scrub is a fail-closed ALLOWLIST, not
# a suffix denylist. Each name is admitted by being listed EXPLICITLY in the supervisor's
# `_INJECTED_KEYS` tuple — NOT by virtue of its suffix. Adding a variable means adding it to that
# allowlist, or the child never sees it (fail closed). All are SERVER-ONLY — read via
# `process.env` in server context, never exposed to the browser (never NEXT_PUBLIC_*).
#
# NOT ALL OF THEM ARE ALWAYS PRESENT. The last two below arrive only when the flight-data
# connector is switched on for this project AND its owner's access has been approved. Read them
# through a check that says so rather than with `!` — `lib/flight-data.reference.ts` has the
# helper, and a missing value otherwise surfaces several layers down as a DNS error.

# app_registry.id — the appId. The app's identity on the platform.
BIAL_APP_ID=

# The portal origin allowed to frame this app — used as the postMessage targetOrigin by
# the error-capture shim and as Caddy's frame-ancestors value. scheme://host[:port], no path.
# e.g. https://low-code-no-code-platform.azurewebsites.net
BIAL_PORTAL_ORIGIN=

# --- per-app Blob object storage -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The app's OWN per-app Blob container: URL + a container-scoped SAS. Read SERVER-SIDE ONLY, and
# accessed directly from the app's own server (never the browser). The container URL is a host the
# sandbox can reach ({sandbox-facing base}/app-<app_id>). NON-secret — safe to log.
BIAL_BLOB_CONTAINER_URL=

# The container-scoped SAS query string (?sv=…&sig=…) the app appends to the container URL. SECRET
# and SERVER-ONLY: never print it, never log it, never persist it to disk, and never send it to the
# browser (not NEXT_PUBLIC_*). It is a bearer capability (read+write+list+delete on this one app's
# container) — the supervisor redacts it from /exec, /dev/logs, and /files output.
BIAL_BLOB_SAS=

# --- per-project database ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This app's OWN PostgreSQL database: a full connection string (postgresql://role:pw@host:port/db)
# for a login role that can reach THIS database and no other — the cross-app wall is a REVOKE
# CONNECT grant the platform makes, not a WHERE clause the app has to remember. Drizzle owns
# everything inside it (schema, migrations, queries); the platform owns only its lifecycle.
# Read by exactly two files: drizzle.config.ts (drizzle-kit generate/migrate) and db/index.ts
# (the server-only pooled client). `npm run dev` applies pending migrations first, non-fatally.
#
# SECRET and SERVER-ONLY, more strictly than anything else on this list: it is a long-lived
# credential, not a TTL'd capability. Read it ONLY in server context (a route handler, a server
# action, drizzle.config.ts) — never in a Client Component, never NEXT_PUBLIC_*, never rendered
# into HTML or window.__BIAL_CONFIG, and never inlined into a tracked file (it would ride the git
# snapshot). The supervisor redacts BOTH the whole value AND its password sub-token from /exec,
# /dev/logs and /files output — but redaction is the accidental-leak guard, not the boundary.
BIAL_DATABASE_URL=

# --- where this app is served from (generated-app access domain) ---------------------------------
# The path this app lives under, e.g. /a/sbx-<28 hex> for a preview or /a/pub-<28 hex> once it is
# published. It is `/a/` plus the container app's own name, so it is derivable rather than looked
# up, and it is STABLE for a published app across redeploys.
#
# WHY A PATH AND NOT A HOSTNAME. Every generated app is served from ONE public hostname and told
# apart by this key, because per-app subdomains would need a wildcard TLS certificate that BIAL
# refused. `next.config.ts` reads this into `basePath`, which is what makes the framework generate
# every link, asset, route handler and the live-reload socket under the key. Nothing in app code
# should read it or hard-code it: Next adds the prefix on its own.
#
# NON-secret. Absent on a plain local dev loop, and the app then serves at `/` exactly as an
# ordinary Next app does. Shape: leading slash, no trailing slash — the supervisor treats any
# other shape as absent rather than passing it on, because this value becomes both a config key
# and a request target.
BIAL_BASE_PATH=

# The single public hostname every generated app is served from, e.g. citizenapps.bialairport.com.
# Read by `next.config.ts` into `experimental.serverActions.allowedOrigins`.
#
# WHY IT IS SEPARATE FROM `allowedDevOrigins`. That setting governs dev asset fetches and the
# reload socket; it does NOT govern Server Actions, which compare the browser's `Origin` against
# the forwarded host and abort on a mismatch. Once traffic arrives through the platform's router
# those two differ by construction — the browser is on this hostname, the upstream Host is the
# container's own name — so without this every form post fails its CSRF check with no other
# symptom. NON-secret.
BIAL_APPS_HOSTNAME=

# --- the flight-data lake (present only when the connector is on for this project) ---------------
# WHERE THE LAKE IS, in one string: `https://<account>.blob.core.windows.net/<container>/<folder>/`.
# It carries the storage account, the one container this app may read, and the folder inside it —
# one value to get wrong instead of three, and it pastes straight into Storage Explorer. Split it
# with `new URL(...)`: `.origin` is the blob endpoint, the first path segment is the container,
# the rest is the prefix. Keep the prefix's trailing slash — a listing prefix is matched as a
# plain string, so `flights` also returns everything under `flights-archive/`.
#
# NON-secret: it is a label. Holding it grants nothing — the credential is the managed identity
# below, which Azure mints inside this container and which cannot be copied out.
BIAL_DICE_URL=

# WHICH IDENTITY MAY READ IT — the managed identity's **client** id, and the single most
# misused value on this list. Pass it explicitly:
#
#   new ManagedIdentityCredential({ clientId: process.env.BIAL_DICE_CLIENT_ID })
#
# NEVER `new DefaultAzureCredential()`. That reads the ambient `AZURE_CLIENT_ID`, which in this
# container belongs to a DIFFERENT identity (the platform's own), so the call is refused with a
# permissions error that sends you reading role assignments for a day. The scrub does not pass
# `AZURE_CLIENT_ID` through at all, which is what makes the mistake fail loudly rather than
# quietly. NON-secret, for the same reason the URL is.
#
# `lib/flight-data.reference.ts` is the worked example: it reads exactly these two variables and
# names the seven mistakes that produce an app which builds green and reports wrong numbers.
BIAL_DICE_CLIENT_ID=

# --- Azure's own, and not ours to set ------------------------------------------------------------
# `IDENTITY_ENDPOINT` and `IDENTITY_HEADER` are injected by Azure Container Apps the moment a
# managed identity is attached to this container. `@azure/identity` reads them for you — you never
# reference either by name. They are on the supervisor's allowlist because the child environment is
# built from an empty dict, so without them the credential cannot reach the token endpoint and the
# failure looks exactly like a missing role assignment. `IDENTITY_HEADER` is a bearer and is
# redacted from /exec, /dev/logs and /files output.
